1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,037
Chapter 29: Operative Vaginal Delivery.

3
00:00:06,037 --> 00:00:21,087
Incidence And Indications Classification And Prerequisites Morbidity Forceps Delivery Vacuum-Assisted Delivery Training References Operative vaginal delivery (OVD) is birth accomplished with assistance from forceps or a vacuum-cup device.

4
00:00:21,087 --> 00:00:28,812
Once these are applied to the fetal head, outward traction generates forces that augment maternal pushing to deliver the fetus.

5
00:00:28,812 --> 00:00:31,325
Incidence And Indications.

6
00:00:31,325 --> 00:00:40,812
From national data, forceps or vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery aided 3.1 percent of births in the United States in 2015.

7
00:00:40,812 --> 00:00:44,600
This is a decline from 9 percent in 1990 .

8
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:53,050
For these procedures, a vacuum is disproportionately selected, and the vacuum-to-forceps delivery ratio approximates 4:1 .

9
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:57,612
Most of these procedures are successful in effecting vaginal birth.

10
00:00:57,612 --> 00:01:09,000
From 2006 United States data, only 0.4 percent of forceps trials and 0.8 percent of vacuum extraction attempts failed to result in vaginal birth .

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:21,187
In the specific group of nulliparas with term gestations, higher failure rates of 4.4 and 6.4 percent, respectively, were found in a study of 25 academic hospitals .

12
00:01:21,187 --> 00:01:31,912
If technically safe, termination of second-stage labor by OVD is considered for any condition that threatens the mother or fetus and that is likely to be relieved by delivery.

13
00:01:31,912 --> 00:01:38,350
Of maternal indications, the most common are maternal exhaustion and prolonged second-stage labor.

14
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:46,475
However, a specific, maximum second-stage length beyond which all women should be considered for OVD has not been defined .

15
00:01:46,475 --> 00:01:54,225
Other maternal reasons are preexisting or intrapartum conditions that limit effective pushing or warrant expedited delivery.

16
00:01:54,225 --> 00:02:04,487
Severe or acute pulmonary compromise, decompensation from intrapartum infection, neurological disease, and serious cardiac disorders are examples.

17
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:11,312
Frequent fetal indications include nonreassuring fetal heart rate and premature placental separation .

18
00:02:11,312 --> 00:02:14,337
Classification And Prerequisites.

19
00:02:14,337 --> 00:02:19,300
Classification of OVD is summarized in Table 29-1.

20
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:26,550
It emphasizes that station and rotation are the two most important discriminators of risk for both mother and neonate.

21
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:36,062
Station is measured by the number of centimeters, either above or below, an anatomical zero station, which is a line drawn between the ischial spines.

22
00:02:36,062 --> 00:02:40,262
Stations range from –5 to 0 to +5.

23
00:02:40,262 --> 00:02:47,250
Procedures are categorized as outlet, low, and midpelvic, and most are low or outlet deliveries.

24
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:54,112
High forceps, in which instruments are applied above 0 station, have no place in current obstetrics.

25
00:02:54,112 --> 00:02:56,662
Table 29-1.

26
00:02:56,662 --> 00:03:11,725
Operative Vaginal Delivery Prerequisites and Classification According to Station and Rotationa To aid patient selection, the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2020) published a preprocedural checklist and documentation template.

27
00:03:11,725 --> 00:03:18,612
Once station and rotation are determined, the prerequisites listed in Table 29-1 are assessed.

28
00:03:18,612 --> 00:03:30,137
For vacuum extraction, fetuses ideally are not younger than 34 weeks’ gestation because of cranial hemorrhage vulnerability at earlier ages (Åberg, 2014).

29
00:03:30,137 --> 00:03:39,150
Also prior to vacuum use, although infrequently used in the United States, fetal scalp blood sampling should not have been recently performed.

30
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:45,412
Of requisites, ascertaining correct head position is essential, and Figure 29-1.

31
00:03:45,412 --> 00:03:47,937
shows fetal skull anatomy.

32
00:03:47,937 --> 00:03:55,050
In unclear cases, sonography can help identify fetal orbits and nasal bridge to aid orientation .

33
00:03:55,050 --> 00:03:57,625
Figure 29-1.

34
00:03:57,625 --> 00:04:04,087
Fetal head (A–B) at term showing fontanels, sutures, and various dimensions.

35
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:09,075
Regional analgesia is preferable for low or midpelvic procedures.

36
00:04:09,075 --> 00:04:13,400
Pudendal blockade may prove adequate for outlet deliveries.

37
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:24,375
As a causative factor, newer epidural methods or placement during early labor do not raise OVD rates (Anim-Somuahm, 2018; Liu, 2004).

38
00:04:24,375 --> 00:04:29,787
The bladder is emptied to provide additional pelvic space and minimize bladder trauma.

39
00:04:29,787 --> 00:04:39,550
Urinary retention and bladder dysfunction are frequent shortterm effects of OVD (Mulder, 2012; Pifarotti, 2014).

40
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:48,450
Notably, episiotomy and epidural analgesia, both common associates of OVD, also are risks for urinary retention.

41
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:54,900
Retention usually resolves after 24 to 48 hours of passive catheter bladder drainage .

42
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:56,797
Morbidity.

43
00:04:56,747 --> 00:04:59,360
# Maternal Morbidity.

44
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:04,910
OVD carries increased risk of certain morbidities for both mother and fetus.

45
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:12,085
In general, a higher station and greater degrees of rotation raise procedure difficulty and the chance of injury.

46
00:05:12,085 --> 00:05:19,322
Morbidity is most properly compared with morbidity from cesarean delivery and not with that from spontaneous birth.

47
00:05:19,322 --> 00:05:26,210
This is the most appropriate comparator because the alternative to indicated OVD is cesarean delivery.

48
00:05:26,210 --> 00:05:37,185
Lacerations The very indications that prompt OVD are among those that also increase the need for episiotomy and the likelihood of lacerations (de Leeuw, 2008).

49
00:05:37,185 --> 00:05:55,660
That said, higher rates of third- and fourth-degree perineal lacerations, vaginal wall lacerations, and less often cervical tears do complicate OVD compared with spontaneous birth (Friedman, 2015; Gurol-Urganci, 2013; Landy, 2011).

50
00:05:55,660 --> 00:06:01,122
These rates with OVD are also substantially higher than with cesarean delivery.

51
00:06:01,122 --> 00:06:07,922
Third- and fourth-degree perineal lacerations are grouped as obstetrical anal sphincter injuries (OASIS).

52
00:06:07,922 --> 00:06:20,947
In general, these tears more frequently attend forceps delivery compared with vacuum extraction, and especially if an episiotomy is midline (Kudish, 2006; O’Mahony, 2010).

53
00:06:20,947 --> 00:06:26,585
With OVD, indicated episiotomy rather than routine use is recommended.

54
00:06:26,585 --> 00:06:40,622
If required, a mediolateral episiotomy affords greater protection against OASIS than midline incision (Gurol-Urganci, 2013; Jangö, 2014; van Bavel, 2018).

55
00:06:40,622 --> 00:06:47,572
But, short-term rates of pain and dyspareunia are similar or increased with mediolateral episiotomy .

56
00:06:47,572 --> 00:06:57,272
As another OASIS protection, early forceps disarticulation and removal can avoid the full perineal distention created by the blades and crowning head.

57
00:06:57,272 --> 00:07:07,710
Moreover, cessation of maternal pushing during disarticulation or as the head begins to crown allows the clinician sole control of outward forces against the perineum.

58
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:15,272
Similarly, adding a dedicated assistant to bolster the perineum can lower OASIS rates, and this is our practice .

59
00:07:15,272 --> 00:07:20,935
Last, OASIS are more common with an occiput posterior (OP) position .

60
00:07:20,935 --> 00:07:29,935
Thus, manual or forceps rotation to an occiput anterior (OA) position may lower rates of lower reproductive tract injury .

61
00:07:29,935 --> 00:07:41,535
Infection and Subsequent-pregnancy Morbidity As detailed in Chapter 30 (p. 548), cesarean delivery carries greater maternal morbidity compared with spontaneous birth.

62
00:07:41,535 --> 00:07:51,047
For OVD, in one study of more than 1 million births, cesarean delivery, but not OVD, was a risk for peripartum hysterectomy .

63
00:07:51,047 --> 00:08:04,497
Further, avoiding cesarean delivery can minimize rates of subsequent pregnancy complications, such as placenta previa, placenta accreta spectrum, uterine rupture, and adhesionrelated surgical organ injury.

64
00:08:04,497 --> 00:08:16,397
In addition, postpartum wound or uterine infection is more frequent in women following cesarean compared with OVD (Bailit, 2016; Halscott, 2015).

65
00:08:16,397 --> 00:08:32,122
Still, to address peripartum infection, investigators in the ANODE trial randomly assigned nearly 3500 gravidas undergoing OVD to receive either placebo or a single intravenous dose of amoxicillin plus clavulanic acid .

66
00:08:32,122 --> 00:08:40,447
The puerperal infection rate was 11 percent after antibiotic prophylaxis compared with 19 percent in those given placebo.

67
00:08:40,447 --> 00:08:50,310
Notably, these infections often coincide with long labors and multiple cervical examinations, both of which are common with women requiring OVD.

68
00:08:50,310 --> 00:08:58,847
Thus, we believe that provision of antibiotics to those undergoing OVD without other infection-related risks warrants further study.

69
00:08:58,847 --> 00:09:12,397
Indeed, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) notes that this prophylaxis is reasonable but recommends against routine antibiotic prophylaxis for all women undergoing OVD.

70
00:09:12,397 --> 00:09:21,647
However, the College (2020b) does note that a single antibiotic dose with third- and fourth-degree perineal laceration is reasonable.

71
00:09:21,647 --> 00:09:30,447
Several studies support this practice (Duggal, 2008; Lewicky-Gaupp, 2015; Stock, 2013a).

72
00:09:30,447 --> 00:09:38,710
Pelvic Floor Disorders This term encompasses urinary incontinence, anal incontinence, and pelvic organ prolapse (POP).

73
00:09:38,710 --> 00:09:42,897
OVD has been implicated as a possible risk for each.

74
00:09:42,897 --> 00:09:48,360
Proposed mechanisms include structural compromise or pelvic floor denervation

75
00:09:48,508 --> 00:09:52,045
secondary to forces exerted during delivery.

76
00:09:51,995 --> 00:10:02,183
For urinary incontinence, parity and specifically vaginal delivery are risk factors (Gyhagen, 2013; Rortveit, 2003).

77
00:10:02,183 --> 00:10:15,470
But, many studies do not support a higher risk from OVD compared with the risk from vaginal birth alone (Gartland, 2016; Leijonhufvud, 2011; MacArthur, 2016).

78
00:10:15,470 --> 00:10:19,958
Evidence linking anal incontinence with OVD is conflicting.

79
00:10:19,958 --> 00:10:31,358
Some studies show that OASIS, but not delivery mode, is the main etiological link (Bols, 2010; Evers, 2012; Nygaard, 1997).

80
00:10:31,358 --> 00:10:40,433
In contrast, others directly associate OVD with anal incontinence (Dolan, 2010; MacArthur, 2013).

81
00:10:40,433 --> 00:10:47,708
But, these studies may not be incongruous—recall that OVD is associated with increased rates of OASIS.

82
00:10:47,708 --> 00:10:55,983
Importantly, several studies and reviews have not found cesarean delivery to be protective long term against anal incontinence .

83
00:10:55,983 --> 00:11:04,795
Similarly for POP, evidence linking it to OVD is mixed (Gyhagen, 2013; Volløyhaug, 2015).

84
00:11:04,795 --> 00:11:10,758
First, pelvic floor mechanisms leading to POP are complex and incompletely defined .

85
00:11:10,758 --> 00:11:28,145
Despite this, obstetrical avulsion of the levator ani muscle from the pelvic sidewall has been implicated, and OVD, especially forceps delivery, is one risk for this injury (DeLancey, 2007; Dietz, 2008; Friedman, 2019).

86
00:11:28,145 --> 00:11:39,420
Again, the pelvic floor injury rather OVD itself may be the causative agent, as those with OVD but no avulsion did not suffer greater POP rates in one recent analysis .

87
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:42,158
# Perinatal Morbidity.

88
00:11:42,158 --> 00:11:49,795
Acute Perinatal Injury OVD carries higher risk for fetal injury than cesarean delivery or spontaneous birth.

89
00:11:49,795 --> 00:12:02,820
Both OVD methods are implicated, and factors can include delivery duration and the number of pulls or cup pop-offs (Åberg, 2019; Levin, 2019; Miller, 2020).

90
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:18,345
Injuries are more frequent with vacuum extraction, and injuries with this device include cephalohematoma, subgaleal hemorrhage, retinal hemorrhage, neonatal jaundice secondary to these hemorrhages, clavicular fracture, and scalp lacerations.

91
00:12:18,345 --> 00:12:27,233
Cephalohematoma and subgaleal hemorrhage are both extracranial lesions illustrated in Figure 33-2 (p. 608).

92
00:12:27,233 --> 00:12:32,983
Shoulder dystocia has been linked to both methods (Dall’Asta, 2016).

93
00:12:32,983 --> 00:12:50,170
Forceps delivery has higher rates of facial nerve injury, brachial plexus injury, depressed skull fracture, and corneal abrasion (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Demissie, 2004; Dupuis, 2005).

94
00:12:50,170 --> 00:13:05,133
With intracranial hemorrhage, some studies have associated vacuum extraction with higher rates, whereas others show similar rates with either OVD method (Towner, 1999; Wen, 2001; Werner, 2011).

95
00:13:05,133 --> 00:13:15,895
Compared with cesarean delivery, OVD carries higher risk of extracranial hematomas, fractures, nerve injuries, retinal hemorrhage, and head lacerations.

96
00:13:15,895 --> 00:13:30,795
However, rates of fetal acidemia or hypoxic encephalopathy are not higher with OVD compared with second-stage cesarean delivery (Bailit, 2016; Contag, 2010; Walsh, 2013).

97
00:13:30,795 --> 00:13:39,483
Intracranial hemorrhage rates are similar among newborns delivered by vacuum extraction, forceps, or cesarean delivery during labor .

98
00:13:39,483 --> 00:13:47,420
But, intracranial hemorrhage rates are higher than among those delivered spontaneously or by cesarean delivery before labor.

99
00:13:47,420 --> 00:13:53,495
These authors suggest that the common risk factor for intracranial hemorrhage is abnormal labor.

100
00:13:53,495 --> 00:14:14,433
Werner and associates (2011), in their evaluation of more than 150,000 singleton deliveries, reported that forceps delivery was associated with fewer total neurological complications that included seizures, intraventricular hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage compared with vacuum-assisted birth or any cesarean delivery.

101
00:14:14,433 --> 00:14:27,945
Compared with rotational OVD, second-stage cesarean delivery has similar maternal and neonatal morbidity rates (Aiken, 2015; Bahl, 2013; Stock, 2013b).

102
00:14:27,945 --> 00:14:44,845
For example, in their large series, Tempest and associates (2013) found similar morbidity rates among malpositioned fetuses during second-stage labor that underwent Kielland rotation, rotational vacuum extraction, or emergency cesarean delivery.

103
00:14:44,845 --> 00:14:49,233
Midforceps by definition are performed from a higher station.

104
00:14:49,233 --> 00:14:55,995
The bulk of data are from older studies, and most show comparable neonatal morbidity between low and

105
00:14:56,164 --> 00:15:02,514
midforceps procedures (Dierker, 1985; Gilstrap, 1984).

106
00:15:02,464 --> 00:15:12,926
In a recent report also comparing these two, Ducarme and coworkers (2015) found comparable neonatal and maternal composite morbidity scores.

107
00:15:12,926 --> 00:15:26,101
Comparing midpelvic OVD and second-stage cesarean delivery, most studies support neonatal safety (Bashore, 1990; Cibils, 1990; HagadornFreathy, 1991).

108
00:15:26,101 --> 00:15:33,389
But, others have found increased rates of trauma and umbilical cord blood acidemia with midforceps delivery .

109
00:15:33,389 --> 00:15:42,339
More recently, Muraca and associates (2018) compared midpelvic OVD methods against second-stage cesarean delivery.

110
00:15:42,339 --> 00:15:48,976
For the indication of dystocia, fetal and maternal morbidity composite scores were higher with OVD.

111
00:15:48,976 --> 00:16:00,551
With distress as the indication, the neonatal morbidity score was greater only with midpelvic vacuum-assisted OVD, whereas the maternal morbidity score was higher only with midforceps.

112
00:16:00,551 --> 00:16:13,264
In another study comparing both midpelvic OVD methods, maternal morbidity was greater with forceps, and neonatal morbidity differed only in a higher cephalohematoma rate with vacuum-assisted delivery .

113
00:16:13,264 --> 00:16:20,489
Mechanisms of Acute Injury The types of fetal injury with OVD can usually be explained by the forces exerted.

114
00:16:20,489 --> 00:16:30,114
In cases of cephalohematoma or subgaleal hemorrhage, suction and perhaps rotation during vacuum extraction may lead to a primary vessel shearing.

115
00:16:30,114 --> 00:16:38,226
Intracranial hemorrhage may result from skull fracture that lacerates vessels or from vessel rupture alone due to exerted forces.

116
00:16:38,226 --> 00:16:44,376
With facial nerve palsy, one of the forceps blades compresses the nerve against the facial bones.

117
00:16:44,376 --> 00:16:56,001
To explain brachial plexus injury, Towner and Ciotti (2007) proposed that as the fetal head descends down the birth canal, the shoulders may stay above the pelvic inlet.

118
00:16:56,001 --> 00:17:07,089
Thus, similar to shoulder dystocia at the symphysis, this “shoulder dystocia at the pelvic inlet” is overcome by traction forces but with concomitant stretch on the brachial plexus.

119
00:17:07,089 --> 00:17:19,201
Long-term Infant Morbidity Evidence regarding long-term neurodevelopmental outcomes in children born by OVD is reassuring (Seidman, 1991; Wesley, 1992).

120
00:17:19,201 --> 00:17:27,989
Moreover, no association between forceps delivery and later epilepsy was found in a cohort study of more than 21,000 births .

121
00:17:27,989 --> 00:17:35,964
In their review, O’Callaghan and colleagues (2011) reported no link between cerebral palsy and OVD.

122
00:17:35,964 --> 00:17:48,964
Last, the incidence of neurodevelopmental morbidity was similar in those undergoing successful forceps delivery, failed forceps with cesarean delivery, or cesarean delivery without forceps in one study .

123
00:17:48,964 --> 00:18:03,064
Similarly, with midpelvic OVD, long-term neurodevelopment is not harmed in most analyses (Dierker, 1986; Friedman, 1984; Nilsen, 1984; Wesley, 1993).

124
00:18:03,064 --> 00:18:11,101
Trial of Operative Vaginal Delivery If an attempt to perform OVD is expected to be difficult, it should be considered a trial.

125
00:18:11,101 --> 00:18:19,826
Moving the woman to an operating room for this attempt, which could be followed by immediate cesarean delivery if OVD fails, has merit.

126
00:18:19,826 --> 00:18:24,901
If forceps cannot be satisfactorily applied, the procedure is stopped.

127
00:18:24,901 --> 00:18:30,489
Either a trial of vacuum-assisted delivery or cesarean delivery is then performed.

128
00:18:30,489 --> 00:18:37,876
With the former, if the fetus does not descend with traction, the trial should be abandoned and cesarean delivery performed.

129
00:18:37,876 --> 00:18:48,051
With such caveats, cesarean delivery after an OVD attempt was not associated with adverse neonatal outcomes if the fetal heart rate was concurrently reassuring .

130
00:18:48,051 --> 00:18:57,439
A similar study evaluated 122 women who had a trial of midpelvic OVD in a setting with full preparations for cesarean delivery .

131
00:18:57,439 --> 00:19:09,289
Investigators found no significant difference in immediate neonatal or maternal morbidity compared with that of 42 women delivered for similar indications by cesarean but without such a trial.

132
00:19:09,289 --> 00:19:20,764
Conversely, in 61 women who had “unexpected” vacuum or forceps failure in which there was no prior preparation for immediate cesarean delivery, neonatal morbidity was higher.

133
00:19:20,764 --> 00:19:32,751
Some factors associated with OVD failure are persistent OP positions and birthweight >4000 g (Ben-Haroush, 2007; Verhoeven, 2016).

134
00:19:32,751 --> 00:19:40,176
However, Palatnik and coworkers (2016) found that risk factors poorly predicted success.

135
00:19:40,176 --> 00:19:43,051
In general, to avert morbidity

136
00:19:43,204 --> 00:19:55,829
with failed OVD, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) cautions that these trials should be attempted only if the clinical assessment suggests a successful outcome.

137
00:19:55,779 --> 00:19:58,704
We also emphasize proper training.

138
00:19:58,704 --> 00:20:06,066
Sequential instrumentation most often involves an attempt at vacuumassisted OVD followed by one with forceps.

139
00:20:06,066 --> 00:20:11,254
This sequence likely reflects the overall higher completion rate with forceps.

140
00:20:11,254 --> 00:20:21,591
However, this practice significantly raises fetal trauma risks (Dupuis, 2005; Gardella, 2001; Murphy, 2011).

141
00:20:21,591 --> 00:20:32,754
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends against the sequential use of instruments unless there is a “compelling and justifiable reason.

142
00:20:32,754 --> 00:20:34,766
Forceps Delivery.

143
00:20:34,766 --> 00:20:36,816
# Design.

144
00:20:36,816 --> 00:20:42,404
Forceps refers to the paired instrument, and each member of this pair is called a branch.

145
00:20:42,404 --> 00:20:50,816
Branches are designated left or right according to the side of the maternal pelvis to which they are applied (Fig. 29-2A).

146
00:20:50,816 --> 00:20:58,004
Each branch has four components: blade, shank, lock, and handle (see Fig. 29-2B).

147
00:20:58,004 --> 00:21:01,866
Each blade has a toe, a heel, and two curves.

148
00:21:01,866 --> 00:21:10,679
Of these, the outward cephalic curve conforms to the round fetal head, whereas the upward pelvic curve corresponds to the curve of the birth canal.

149
00:21:10,679 --> 00:21:15,016
Some blades are solid, meaning smooth on both surfaces.

150
00:21:15,016 --> 00:21:22,941
Others have an opening within or a depression along the blade surface and are termed fenestrated or pseudofenestrated, respectively.

151
00:21:22,941 --> 00:21:29,254
A true window in the blade reduces the degree of head slippage within the blades during forceps rotation.

152
00:21:29,254 --> 00:21:35,079
Disadvantageously, it can increase blade thickness and friction against the vaginal wall.

153
00:21:35,079 --> 00:21:42,466
With pseudofenestration, the forceps blade is smooth on the outer maternal side but indented on the inner fetal surface.

154
00:21:42,466 --> 00:21:49,179
The goal is to reduce head slipping yet improve the ease and safety of application and removal of forceps.

155
00:21:49,179 --> 00:21:55,116
In most situations, however, despite these subtle differences, any are appropriate.

156
00:21:55,116 --> 00:21:57,666
Figure 29-2.

157
00:21:57,666 --> 00:22:03,516
A. Simpson forceps have fenestrated blades, parallel shanks, and English lock.

158
00:22:03,516 --> 00:22:06,866
The cephalic curve accommodates the fetal head.

159
00:22:06,866 --> 00:22:14,591
B. Luikart forceps have pseudofenestrated blades, overlapping shanks, a sliding lock, and tongue-in-groove handles.

160
00:22:14,591 --> 00:22:18,704
The pelvic curve (black curve) is marked in this example.

161
00:22:18,704 --> 00:22:23,629
The blades are connected to shanks, which may be parallel or overlapping.

162
00:22:23,629 --> 00:22:31,154
Parallel shanks limit compression of blades against the fetal head and may be helpful with deliveries employing greater forces.

163
00:22:31,154 --> 00:22:35,266
These shanks, however, add width against the introitus.

164
00:22:35,266 --> 00:22:41,466
In comparison, overlapping shanks raise compression forces but distend the perineum less.

165
00:22:41,466 --> 00:22:45,479
This may offer greatest advantage for outlet deliveries.

166
00:22:45,479 --> 00:22:52,129
Locks are found on all forceps and help to connect the right and left branches and stabilize the instrument.

167
00:22:52,129 --> 00:23:01,529
They can be located at the end of the shank nearest to the handles (English lock), at the ends of the handles (pivot lock), or along the shank (sliding lock).

168
00:23:01,529 --> 00:23:08,141
Although varied in design, handles, when squeezed, raise compression forces against the fetal head.

169
00:23:08,141 --> 00:23:12,629
Thus, forces to consider include traction and compression.

170
00:23:12,629 --> 00:23:15,804
# Blade Application and Delivery.

171
00:23:15,804 --> 00:23:20,941
Forceps blades grasp the head and are applied according to fetal head position.

172
00:23:20,941 --> 00:23:31,979
If the head is in an OA position, two or more fingers of the right hand are introduced inside the left posterior portion of the vulva and advanced into the vagina beside the fetal head.

173
00:23:31,979 --> 00:23:37,254
The handle of the left branch is grasped between the thumb and two fingers of the left hand .

174
00:23:37,254 --> 00:23:42,554
The blade tip is then gently introduced along the posterior left vaginal wall.

175
00:23:42,554 --> 00:23:48,129
It is swept upward and inward between the fetal head and the palmar surface of the fingers .

176
00:23:48,129 --> 00:23:51,716
This blade can be held in position by an assistant.

177
00:23:51,716 --> 00:24:01,679
For application of the right blade, two or more fingers of the left hand are introduced into the right posterior portion of the vagina to serve as a guide for the right blade.

178
00:24:01,679 --> 00:24:05,979
This blade is held in the right hand and introduced into the vagina.

179
00:24:05,979 --> 00:24:12,741
With each blade, the thumb is positioned behind the heel, and most of the insertion force comes from this thumb .

180
00:24:12,741 --> 00:24:17,741
If the head is positioned in a left OA (LOA) or right OA

181
00:24:17,908 --> 00:24:23,170
ROA) position, then the lower of the two blades is typically placed first.

182
00:24:23,120 --> 00:24:25,720
Figure 29-3.

183
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:31,658
For OA or LOA positions, the left handle of the forceps is held in the left hand.

184
00:24:31,658 --> 00:24:38,770
The blade is introduced into the left side of the pelvis between the fetal head and the fingers of the operator’s right hand.

185
00:24:38,770 --> 00:24:41,433
Figure 29-4.

186
00:24:41,433 --> 00:24:43,845
Insertion arc of the blade.

187
00:24:43,845 --> 00:24:49,733
Importantly, the thumb of the right hand, not shown here, guides the blade during placement.

188
00:24:49,733 --> 00:24:52,470
Figure 29-5.

189
00:24:52,470 --> 00:24:57,983
In applying the second blade, insertional force is generated mainly by the thumb.

190
00:24:57,983 --> 00:25:03,133
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

191
00:25:03,133 --> 00:25:12,445
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

192
00:25:12,445 --> 00:25:16,933
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

193
00:25:16,933 --> 00:25:23,233
The blades are constructed so that their cephalic curve is closely adapted to the sides of the fetal head .

194
00:25:23,233 --> 00:25:32,658
The fetal head is perfectly grasped only when the long axis of the blades corresponds to the occipitomental diameter (see Fig. 29-1).

195
00:25:32,658 --> 00:25:36,995
As a result, most of the blade lies over the lateral face.

196
00:25:36,995 --> 00:25:39,670
Figure 29-6.

197
00:25:39,670 --> 00:25:43,908
A. The forceps are symmetrically placed and articulated.

198
00:25:43,908 --> 00:25:46,483
B. The vertex is OA.

199
00:25:46,483 --> 00:25:51,633
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

200
00:25:51,633 --> 00:26:00,945
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

201
00:26:00,945 --> 00:26:05,433
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

202
00:26:05,433 --> 00:26:09,170
Suboptimal blade placement can increase morbidity .

203
00:26:09,170 --> 00:26:18,870
For OA positions, appropriately applied blades are equidistant from the sagittal suture, and each blade is equidistant from its adjacent lambdoid suture.

204
00:26:18,870 --> 00:26:25,970
In OP positions, blades are symmetrically placed relative to the sagittal suture and to each coronal suture.

205
00:26:25,970 --> 00:26:30,195
After correct positioning, the branches can be articulated.

206
00:26:30,195 --> 00:26:35,895
If branches do not articulate, the blade’s relationships to the sutures are reassessed.

207
00:26:35,895 --> 00:26:42,245
Applied in this way, forceps should not slip, and traction may be applied most advantageously.

208
00:26:42,245 --> 00:26:53,045
With most forceps, if one blade is applied over the brow and the other over the occiput, the instrument cannot be locked, or if locked, the blades will slip off when traction is applied.

209
00:26:53,045 --> 00:27:00,258
With both branches in place, slow articulation of the handles will also typically correct mild asynclitism.

210
00:27:00,258 --> 00:27:09,745
For greater asynclitism, Luikart forceps can be placed and once positioned, one handle will extend further than the other along their long axis.

211
00:27:09,745 --> 00:27:19,495
Asynclitism is resolved by pulling and/or pushing each respective branch within the sliding lock along the instrument’s long axis until the finger guards align.

212
00:27:19,495 --> 00:27:26,483
Once blades are placed satisfactorily, rotation to OA position is performed before traction is applied .

213
00:27:26,483 --> 00:27:35,408
Next, gentle, intermittent, downward and outward traction is exerted concurrent with maternal efforts until the perineum begins to bulge.

214
00:27:35,408 --> 00:27:41,945
For most stations, the initial direction of traction is downward and brings the head beneath the symphysis.

215
00:27:41,945 --> 00:27:44,620
Figure 29-7.

216
00:27:44,620 --> 00:27:49,908
If LOA, the vertex is rotated from this position to OA (arrow).

217
00:27:49,908 --> 00:27:55,058
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

218
00:27:55,058 --> 00:28:04,370
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

219
00:28:04,370 --> 00:28:08,858
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

220
00:28:08,858 --> 00:28:15,070
With head descent, the vector of forces changes continuously and are gradually directed upward .

221
00:28:15,070 --> 00:28:22,083
During the birth of the head, mechanisms of spontaneous delivery should be simulated as closely as possible.

222
00:28:22,083 --> 00:28:29,233
As a teaching tool for this, a Bill axis traction device can be attached over the finger guards of most forceps.

223
00:28:29,233 --> 00:28:32,808
The instrument has an arrow and indicator line.

224
00:28:32,808 --> 00:28:38,670
When the arrow points directly to the line, traction is along the path of least resistance.

225
00:28:38,670 --> 00:28:41,195
Figure 29-8.

226
00:28:41,195 --> 00:28:47,670
With low forceps, the direction of gentle traction for delivery of the head is indicated (arrows).

227
00:28:47,670 --> 00:28:52,820
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

228
00:28:52,820 --> 00:29:02,133
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

229
00:29:02,133 --> 00:29:06,620
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

230
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:12,733
It is impossible to ascertain the amount of force exerted by forceps for an individual patient.

231
00:29:12,733 --> 00:29:15,458
Thus, traction is ideally

232
00:29:15,628 --> 00:29:20,853
intermittent and relieved between contractions, as in spontaneous labor.

233
00:29:20,803 --> 00:29:29,003
Except when urgently indicated, delivery should be sufficiently slow, deliberate, and gentle to prevent undue head compression.

234
00:29:29,003 --> 00:29:33,678
It is preferable to apply traction only with each uterine contraction.

235
00:29:33,678 --> 00:29:36,915
Maternal pushing will augment these efforts.

236
00:29:36,915 --> 00:29:43,353
With traction, as the occiput distends the vulva, an episiotomy may be performed if needed.

237
00:29:43,353 --> 00:29:46,790
The delivery may be completed in several ways.

238
00:29:46,790 --> 00:29:50,928
Some clinicians keep the forceps in place to control the head.

239
00:29:50,928 --> 00:29:58,165
However, this blade volume adds to vulvar distention and raises risks for laceration or episiotomy.

240
00:29:58,165 --> 00:30:04,528
To prevent this, the forceps may be removed, and delivery is then completed by maternal pushing .

241
00:30:04,528 --> 00:30:11,715
Importantly, if blades are disarticulated and removed too early, the head may recede and prolong delivery.

242
00:30:11,715 --> 00:30:16,278
Pushing in some cases may be aided by the modified Ritgen maneuver.

243
00:30:16,278 --> 00:30:21,078
We emphasize bolstering of the perineum to control outward forces.

244
00:30:21,078 --> 00:30:23,690
Figure 29-9.

245
00:30:23,690 --> 00:30:28,890
Branches are removed in the opposite order from that in which they were originally placed.

246
00:30:28,890 --> 00:30:34,228
The fingers of the right hand, covered by a sterile towel, bolster the perineum.

247
00:30:34,228 --> 00:30:38,690
The thumb is placed directly on the head to prevent sudden egress.

248
00:30:38,690 --> 00:30:43,840
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

249
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:53,153
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

250
00:30:53,153 --> 00:30:57,640
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

251
00:30:57,640 --> 00:31:00,290
# Manual Rotation.

252
00:31:00,290 --> 00:31:07,740
In many cases, OT and OP head positions are often imperfectly flexed and present a wider head diameter.

253
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:11,940
This can slow fetal descent and lengthen second-stage labor.

254
00:31:11,940 --> 00:31:21,865
By manually rotating the fetal occiput into the anterior pelvis (OA positions), cephalopelvic proportions improve to help hasten spontaneous birth.

255
00:31:21,865 --> 00:31:25,440
This is usually reserved for second-stage labor.

256
00:31:25,440 --> 00:31:39,828
Compared with no attempt, manual rotation is associated with lower rates of cesarean delivery, OVD, severe perineal laceration, and chorioamnionitis (Blanc, 2021; Shaffer, 2011).

257
00:31:39,828 --> 00:31:45,753
If OVD is still required, the new OA position lowers rates of perineal trauma.

258
00:31:45,753 --> 00:31:51,528
In the simplest cases, digital rotation can correct OT or OP positioning.

259
00:31:51,528 --> 00:31:56,715
With head molding, the sutures override one another to create a bony ridge.

260
00:31:56,715 --> 00:32:04,553
Two fingers hooked against this ridge and forces directed parallel to the skull can rotate the occiput toward the anterior pelvis.

261
00:32:04,553 --> 00:32:10,265
This is done between contractions, and advancements are then held in place during pushing.

262
00:32:10,265 --> 00:32:19,365
Progressive degrees of rotation are attained with each attempt, and several may be needed to ultimately rotate the occiput into the anterior pelvis.

263
00:32:19,365 --> 00:32:24,878
Instead, with manual rotation, an open hand is inserted into the vagina.

264
00:32:24,878 --> 00:32:30,828
For ROP position, the right palm cups the fetal head and straddles the sagittal suture.

265
00:32:30,828 --> 00:32:37,453
The operator’s fingers wrap to one side of the fetal face, and the thumb extends along the other side.

266
00:32:37,453 --> 00:32:42,190
Rotation is clockwise to bring the occiput to an ROA position .

267
00:32:42,190 --> 00:32:47,265
With LOP position, rotation is counterclockwise with the left palm.

268
00:32:47,265 --> 00:32:51,890
Three actions are performed simultaneously between contractions.

269
00:32:51,890 --> 00:32:58,765
The fetal head is flexed to provide a smaller diameter for rotation and subsequent descent within the pelvis.

270
00:32:58,765 --> 00:33:06,928
Next, slight destationing of the fetal head moves the head to a level in the maternal pelvis with sufficient room to complete the rotation.

271
00:33:06,928 --> 00:33:13,578
Importantly, destationing should not be confused with disengaging the fetal head, which is proscribed.

272
00:33:13,578 --> 00:33:25,665
Third, we and others prefer also to place the other hand externally on the corresponding side of the maternal abdomen to pull the back of the fetus up and toward the midline in synchrony with internal head rotation.

273
00:33:25,665 --> 00:33:31,028
Barth (2015) provides an excellent summary of this technique.

274
00:33:31,028 --> 00:33:33,590
Figure 29-10.

275
00:33:33,590 --> 00:33:39,115
A. Manual rotation using the left hand, palm-up, to rotate from ROP.

276
00:33:39,115 --> 00:33:45,203
B. The head is flexed and destationed during clockwise rotation to reach an OA position.

277
00:33:45,203 --> 00:33:50,353
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

278
00:33:50,353 --> 00:33:59,665
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

279
00:33:59,665 --> 00:34:04,153
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

280
00:34:04,153 --> 00:34:05,940
Le Ray and

281
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:14,425
associates (2007, 2013) reported a success rate >90 percent with manual rotation.

282
00:34:14,375 --> 00:34:16,875
Complication were uncommon.

283
00:34:16,875 --> 00:34:22,050
In their report of 796 rotations, no cords prolapsed.

284
00:34:22,050 --> 00:34:27,800
Two women sustained cervical lacerations, which required simple vaginal repair.

285
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:37,300
Notably, this study allowed rotations at advanced cervical dilation prior to second-stage labor, which may have enhanced cervical injury risk.

286
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:47,312
In 71 percent, fetal heart rate (FHR) was normal during rotation, but severe FHR abnormalities were noted in 10 percent.

287
00:34:47,312 --> 00:34:55,675
However, the need for cesarean delivery after successful manual rotation was not associated with FHR abnormalities.

288
00:34:55,675 --> 00:35:02,037
Failure to rotate the head does not mandate cesarean delivery, and options are described next.

289
00:35:02,037 --> 00:35:06,037
# Persistent Occiput Posterior Positions.

290
00:35:06,037 --> 00:35:19,150
In cases with persistent OP positioning, the head may spontaneously deliver OP, may be manually or instrumentally rotated to an OA position, or may be delivered OP by forceps or vacuum .

291
00:35:19,150 --> 00:35:27,362
In many cases, the cause of a persistent OP position and of the difficulty in accomplishing rotation is an anthropoid pelvis.

292
00:35:27,362 --> 00:35:35,625
This architecture opposes rotation and predisposes to posterior delivery (Fig. 2-16, p. 29).

293
00:35:35,625 --> 00:35:44,062
With forceps delivery from an OP position, downward and outward traction is applied until the base of the nose passes under the symphysis .

294
00:35:44,062 --> 00:35:50,862
The handles are then slowly elevated until the occiput gradually emerges over the perineum’s fourchette.

295
00:35:50,862 --> 00:35:57,975
The forceps are directed downward again, and the nose, mouth, and chin successively emerge from the vulva.

296
00:35:57,975 --> 00:36:00,637
Figure 29-11.

297
00:36:00,637 --> 00:36:04,250
Outlet forceps delivery from an OP position.

298
00:36:04,250 --> 00:36:08,700
The head should be flexed after the bregma passes under the symphysis.

299
00:36:08,700 --> 00:36:14,425
OP delivery causes greater vulvar distention, and an episiotomy may be needed.

300
00:36:14,425 --> 00:36:26,462
OP deliveries have a higher incidence of severe perineal lacerations and of extensive episiotomy compared with OA positions (de Leeuw, 2008; Pearl, 1993).

301
00:36:26,462 --> 00:36:38,325
Also, newborns undergoing OVD from OP positions have a higher incidence of Erb and facial nerve palsies, 1 and 2 percent, respectively, than those delivered from OA positions.

302
00:36:38,325 --> 00:36:44,000
As expected, rotations to OA ultimately decrease perineal delivery trauma .

303
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:52,312
For forceps rotations from an OP to OA position, Kielland forceps are preferred because they have a less pronounced pelvic curve .

304
00:36:52,312 --> 00:37:01,425
Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition offers a more detailed description and illustration of this Kielland forceps procedure .

305
00:37:01,425 --> 00:37:04,100
Figure 29-12.

306
00:37:04,100 --> 00:37:06,275
Kielland forceps.

307
00:37:06,275 --> 00:37:13,000
The characteristic features are minimal pelvic curvature (A), sliding lock (B), and light weight.

308
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:16,325
# Occiput Transverse Positions.

309
00:37:16,325 --> 00:37:22,587
With occiput transverse (OT) fetal head positions, rotation is required for delivery.

310
00:37:22,587 --> 00:37:28,575
For this, the head may be manually or instrumentally rotated into an OA position.

311
00:37:28,575 --> 00:37:33,962
Manual rotation was described earlier in that section (p. 540).

312
00:37:33,962 --> 00:37:44,575
Forceps rotation by experienced operators can offer high success rates with minimal maternal morbidity (Burke, 2012; Stock, 2013b).

313
00:37:44,575 --> 00:37:50,737
Either standard forceps or specialized forceps, such as Kielland forceps, are used.

314
00:37:50,737 --> 00:37:57,625
With the latter, each handle has a small knob, and branches are placed so that this knob faces the occiput.

315
00:37:57,625 --> 00:38:01,987
Kielland described two methods of applying the anterior blade.

316
00:38:01,987 --> 00:38:07,425
In this example, placement with a left OT (LOT) position is described.

317
00:38:07,425 --> 00:38:13,400
With the wandering method, the anterior blade is first introduced into the posterior pelvis .

318
00:38:13,400 --> 00:38:17,662
The blade is then arched around the face to an anterior position.

319
00:38:17,662 --> 00:38:24,087
To permit this sweep of the blade, the handle is held close to the left maternal buttock throughout the maneuver.

320
00:38:24,087 --> 00:38:28,912
The second blade is introduced posteriorly, and the branches are locked.

321
00:38:28,912 --> 00:38:31,762
Figure 29-13.

322
00:38:31,762 --> 00:38:37,187
A. Application of the right branch of the Kielland forceps to a head in LOT position.

323
00:38:37,187 --> 00:38:42,200
The knob on this branch (colored blue) will ultimately face the occiput.

324
00:38:42,200 --> 00:38:47,112
B. The right branch is wandered to its final position behind the symphysis.

325
00:38:47,112 --> 00:38:54,087
C. Insertion of the left branch of the Kielland forceps directly posterior along the hollow of the sacrum.

326
00:38:54,087 --> 00:39:00,587
This branch is inserted to the maternal right of the anterior branch to aid in engaging the sliding lock.

327
00:39:00,587 --> 00:39:02,375
Reproduced

328
00:39:02,524 --> 00:39:07,024
with permission from Yeomans ER: Operative vaginal delivery.

329
00:39:06,974 --> 00:39:16,286
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

330
00:39:16,286 --> 00:39:20,774
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

331
00:39:20,774 --> 00:39:31,636
After checking the application, the provider pulls the handles of the Kielland forceps slightly to the patient’s right to flex the fetal head and create a smaller diameter for rotation.

332
00:39:31,636 --> 00:39:38,036
The first and second fingers of the left hand are placed over the finger guards with the palm against the handles.

333
00:39:38,036 --> 00:39:41,024
This palm faces the maternal left.

334
00:39:41,024 --> 00:39:48,186
Concurrently, the first two fingers of the operator’s right hand are placed against the anterior lambdoid suture.

335
00:39:48,186 --> 00:39:52,161
The fetal head is then elevated and slightly destationed.

336
00:39:52,161 --> 00:39:59,599
For rotation in a counterclockwise direction, the wrist of the left hand supinates, to direct this palm upward.

337
00:39:59,599 --> 00:40:05,661
Simultaneously, two fingers of the right hand press against the lambdoid suture ridge.

338
00:40:05,661 --> 00:40:10,374
This ensures that the fetal head turns with the blades and does not slip.

339
00:40:10,374 --> 00:40:16,011
As a newer adjunct, abdominal sonography can follow and document head rotation .

340
00:40:16,011 --> 00:40:24,286
The second type of blade application introduces the anterior blade with its cephalic curve directed upward to curve under the symphysis.

341
00:40:24,286 --> 00:40:33,999
After it has been advanced far enough toward the upper vagina, it is turned on its long axis through 180 degrees to adapt the cephalic curvature to the head.

342
00:40:33,999 --> 00:40:38,824
The second blade is introduced posteriorly, and the branches are locked.

343
00:40:38,824 --> 00:40:46,211
After rotation of the fetal occiput into the anterior pelvis, two acceptable methods for delivery are available.

344
00:40:46,211 --> 00:40:56,349
In one, the operator applies traction on the Kielland forceps using a bimanual grip described previously for conventional forceps (p. 539).

345
00:40:56,349 --> 00:41:04,461
When the posterior fontanel has passed under the subpubic arch, the handles can be elevated to the horizontal, but no further.

346
00:41:04,461 --> 00:41:11,249
Raising the handles above the horizontal may cause vaginal sulcus tears because of the reverse pelvic curve .

347
00:41:11,249 --> 00:41:18,449
Alternatively, the Kielland forceps can be removed after rotation and replaced with conventional forceps.

348
00:41:18,449 --> 00:41:24,636
With this approach, moderate traction is first employed to seat the head before switching instruments.

349
00:41:24,636 --> 00:41:27,399
# Face Presentations.

350
00:41:27,399 --> 00:41:32,199
With a mentum anterior face presentation, OVD is an option.

351
00:41:32,199 --> 00:41:41,586
The blades are applied to the sides of the head along the occipitomental diameter, with the pelvic curve directed toward the neck (see Fig. 29-1).

352
00:41:41,586 --> 00:41:46,236
Downward traction is exerted until the chin appears under the symphysis.

353
00:41:46,236 --> 00:41:57,261
Then, by an upward movement, the face is slowly extracted, with the nose, eyes, brow, and occiput appearing in succession over the anterior margin of the perineum.

354
00:41:57,261 --> 00:42:05,799
Forceps should not be applied to the mentum posterior presentation because vaginal delivery is impossible except in very small fetuses.

355
00:42:05,799 --> 00:42:08,224
Vacuum-Assisted Delivery.

356
00:42:08,224 --> 00:42:11,249
# Vacuum Extractor Design.

357
00:42:11,249 --> 00:42:19,711
With vacuum-assisted OVD, suction is created within a cup placed on the fetal scalp such that traction on the cup aids fetal birth.

358
00:42:19,711 --> 00:42:26,974
In the United States, vacuum extractor is the preferred term, whereas in Europe it is commonly called a ventouse .

359
00:42:26,974 --> 00:42:39,349
Theoretical benefits of this tool compared with forceps include simpler requirements for precise positioning on the fetal head and avoidance of space-occupying blades within the vagina to help mitigate maternal trauma.

360
00:42:39,349 --> 00:42:42,174
Figure 29-14.

361
00:42:42,174 --> 00:42:44,749
Vacuum delivery systems.

362
00:42:44,749 --> 00:42:53,849
A. The Kiwi OmniCup contains a handheld vacuumgenerating pump, which is attached via flexible tubing to a rigid plastic mushroom cup.

363
00:42:53,849 --> 00:43:01,949
B. The Mityvac Mystic II MitySoft Bell Cup has a soft bell cup attached by a semirigid shaft to a handheld pump.

364
00:43:01,949 --> 00:43:07,274
Vacuum devices contain a cup, shaft, handle, and vacuum generator.

365
00:43:07,274 --> 00:43:13,149
Vacuum cups are metal or plastic and differ in their shape, size, and reusability.

366
00:43:13,149 --> 00:43:17,436
In the United States, plastic cups are generally preferred.

367
00:43:17,436 --> 00:43:27,124
Of these, the soft cup is a pliable bell-shaped dome, whereas the rigid type has a firm flattened mushroom-shaped cup and circular ridge around the cup rim .

368
00:43:27,124 --> 00:43:36,311
When compared, rigid mushroom cups generate significantly more traction force (Hofmeyr, 1990; Muise, 1993).

369
00:43:36,311 --> 00:43:41,586
With OP positions or with asynclitism, the flatter cup also permits

370
00:43:41,740 --> 00:43:47,752
improved placement at the flexion point, which is typically less accessible with these head positions.

371
00:43:47,702 --> 00:43:52,327
The trade-off is that the flatter cups have higher scalp laceration rates.

372
00:43:52,327 --> 00:43:58,840
Thus, many manufacturers recommend soft bell cups for more straightforward OA deliveries.

373
00:43:58,840 --> 00:44:01,365
Table 29-2.

374
00:44:01,365 --> 00:44:09,202
Vacuum Cups for Operative Vaginal Delivery Several investigators have compared outcomes with various rigid and soft cups.

375
00:44:09,202 --> 00:44:18,127
Metal cups yield higher success rates but greater rates of scalp injuries, including cephalohematomas (O’Mahony, 2010).

376
00:44:18,127 --> 00:44:27,302
In another study, Kuit and colleagues (1993) found that the only advantage of the soft cups was a lower incidence of scalp injury.

377
00:44:27,302 --> 00:44:40,377
In a review, Vacca (2002) concluded that fewer scalp lacerations occurred with the soft cup, but that the rate of cephalohematomas and subgaleal hemorrhage was similar between soft and rigid cups.

378
00:44:40,377 --> 00:44:46,590
Importantly, high-pressure vacuum generates large amounts of force regardless of the cup used .

379
00:44:46,590 --> 00:44:52,690
Aside from the cup, the shaft that connects the cup and handle may be flexible or semiflexible.

380
00:44:52,690 --> 00:45:00,190
Tubing-like flexible shafts may be preferred for OP positions or asynclitism to permit better seating of the cup.

381
00:45:00,190 --> 00:45:08,352
Last, the vacuum generator may be handheld and actuated by the operator, or it may be held and operated by an assistant.

382
00:45:08,352 --> 00:45:10,515
# Technique.

383
00:45:10,515 --> 00:45:16,440
An important step in vacuum-assisted OVD is proper cup placement over the flexion point.

384
00:45:16,440 --> 00:45:26,127
This pivot point maximizes traction, minimizes cup detachment, flexes the neck, and delivers the smallest head diameter through the pelvic outlet.

385
00:45:26,127 --> 00:45:35,077
This improves success rates, lowers scalp injury rates, and lessens perineal trauma because the smallest head diameter distends the vulva .

386
00:45:35,077 --> 00:45:46,715
The flexion point is found along the sagittal suture, approximately 3 cm from the posterior fontanel’s center and approximately 6 cm from the anterior fontanel’s center.

387
00:45:46,715 --> 00:45:58,540
Because most cup diameters measure 5 to 6 cm, when properly placed, the cup rim lies at the posterior fontanel’s border and 3 cm from the anterior fontanel .

388
00:45:58,540 --> 00:46:09,752
Placement of the cup more anteriorly on the fetal cranium—near the anterior fontanel—is ideally avoided as it leads to neck extension during traction unless the fetus is small.

389
00:46:09,752 --> 00:46:14,627
Such placement delivers a wider head diameter through the vaginal opening.

390
00:46:14,627 --> 00:46:20,815
Last, asymmetrical placement relative to the sagittal suture may worsen asynclitism.

391
00:46:20,815 --> 00:46:24,740
Cup placement in OA positions is seldom difficult.

392
00:46:24,740 --> 00:46:33,352
In contrast, if the indication for delivery is failure to descend caused by occipital malposition, cup positioning can be difficult.

393
00:46:33,352 --> 00:46:36,140
Figure 29-15.

394
00:46:36,140 --> 00:46:40,415
Drawing demonstrates correct cup placement at the flexion point.

395
00:46:40,415 --> 00:46:49,290
Along the sagittal suture, this spot lies 3 cm from the posterior fontanel and 6 cm from the anterior fontanel.

396
00:46:49,290 --> 00:46:59,840
During cup placement, maternal soft tissue entrapment predisposes the mother to lacerations and virtually ensures cup dislodgement, colloquially called a “pop off.

397
00:46:59,840 --> 00:47:09,840
Thus, the entire cup circumference should be palpated both before and after the vacuum has been created and again prior to traction to exclude such entrapment.

398
00:47:09,840 --> 00:47:25,740
Gradual vacuum creation is advocated by some and is generated by increasing the suction in increments of 0.2 kg/cm2 every 2 minutes until a total negative pressure of 0.8 kg/cm2 is reached .

399
00:47:25,740 --> 00:47:41,902
That said, other studies have shown that negative pressure can be increased to 0.8 kg/cm2 in <2 minutes without a significant difference in efficacy or in maternal and fetal outcomes (Suwannachat, 2011, 2012).

400
00:47:41,902 --> 00:47:44,452
Table 29-3.

401
00:47:44,452 --> 00:47:51,827
Vacuum Pressure Conversions Once suction is created, the instrument handle is grasped, and traction is initiated.

402
00:47:51,827 --> 00:47:59,790
Similar to forceps delivery, traction angles mirror that in Figure 298 and should be steady without jerking or rocking.

403
00:47:59,790 --> 00:48:05,502
The number of needed pulls increases with higher station but typically ranges from one to four.

404
00:48:05,502 --> 00:48:10,577
Efforts are intermittent, and each pull is coordinated with maternal pushing.

405
00:48:10,577 --> 00:48:20,565
Manual torque to the cup is avoided as it can cause cup displacement or cephalohematomas and, with metal cups, “cookie-cutter”–type scalp lacerations.

406
00:48:20,565 --> 00:48:25,477
Thus, OA oblique positions are corrected not by rotation, but

407
00:48:25,636 --> 00:48:28,648
solely by downward outward traction.

408
00:48:28,598 --> 00:48:33,861
Similarly, for OP positions, rotational forces should not be applied.

409
00:48:33,861 --> 00:48:43,348
Instead, with correct flexion point placement and line of traction, the head will usually rotate by itself (van den Akker, 2019).

410
00:48:43,348 --> 00:48:52,786
During pulls, the operator should place the nondominant hand within the vagina, with the thumb on the extractor cup and one or more fingers on the fetal scalp.

411
00:48:52,786 --> 00:48:59,661
So positioned, descent of the presenting part can be judged and the traction angle can be adjusted with head descent.

412
00:48:59,661 --> 00:49:06,286
In addition, the relationship of the cup edge to the scalp can be assessed to help detect cup separation.

413
00:49:06,286 --> 00:49:18,473
Between contractions, some physicians will lower the suction levels to decrease scalp injury rates, whereas others will maintain suction in cases with a nonreassuring FHR to aid rapid delivery.

414
00:49:18,473 --> 00:49:28,073
No differences in maternal or fetal outcome were noted if the vacuum level was decreased between contractions or if an effort was made to prevent fetal loss of station .

415
00:49:28,073 --> 00:49:33,223
Once the head is delivered, the vacuum pressure is relieved and the cup removed.

416
00:49:33,223 --> 00:49:36,598
Vacuum extraction should be considered a trial.

417
00:49:36,598 --> 00:49:43,548
Without early and clear evidence of descent toward delivery, an alternative delivery approach should be adopted.

418
00:49:43,548 --> 00:49:48,998
As a general guideline, progressive descent should accompany each traction attempt.

419
00:49:48,998 --> 00:50:00,923
Neither data nor consensus are available regarding the number of pulls required to effect delivery, the maximum number of cup popoffs that can be tolerated, or optimal total duration of the procedure.

420
00:50:00,923 --> 00:50:09,948
Some manufacturers have recommendations regarding these (Clinical Innovations, 2018; CooperSurgical, 2018).

421
00:50:09,948 --> 00:50:23,548
During a vacuum-assisted delivery trial, cup dislodgement due to technical failure or less than optimal placement should not be equated with dislodgement under ideal conditions of exact cup placement and optimal vacuum maintenance.

422
00:50:23,548 --> 00:50:35,061
Technical failures may merit either additional attempts at cup placement or, alternatively, a trial of forceps (Ezenagu, 1999; Williams, 1991).

423
00:50:35,061 --> 00:50:44,773
The least desirable cases are those in which traction without progress or multiple disengagements occur following correct cup application and appropriate traction.

424
00:50:44,773 --> 00:50:53,886
As with forceps, clinicians should embrace a willingness to abandon attempts at vacuum-assisted delivery if satisfactory progress is not made .

425
00:50:53,886 --> 00:50:55,348
Training.

426
00:50:55,348 --> 00:51:04,861
As the OVD rate has declined, so have opportunities for resident training (Fitzwater, 2015; Kyser, 2014).

427
00:51:04,861 --> 00:51:22,386
In accredited programs, the median number of these procedures completed by trainees has reached critically low levels, and for recent graduates it approximated only 20 (Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education, 2019; Dildy, 2016).

428
00:51:22,386 --> 00:51:33,461
For residents completing training in 2021, the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (2020) minimum requirement is 15 procedures.

429
00:51:33,461 --> 00:51:42,861
To adapt, residency programs should have readily available skilled operators to teach these procedures by simulation as well as through actual cases .

430
00:51:42,861 --> 00:51:55,136
In one study, a 59-percent increase in forceps deliveries over 2 years was related to a single experienced and proactive instructor assigned to teach forceps to residents in labor and delivery .

431
00:51:55,136 --> 00:52:09,736
In another, following implementation of a formal education program that included a manikin and pelvic model simulation, rates of neonatal morbidity and of severe maternal cervical, labial, and vaginal lacerations declined.

432
00:52:09,736 --> 00:52:13,098
However, OASIS rates were unchanged .

433
00:52:13,098 --> 00:52:22,123
Gossett and associates (2016) found a 22-percent reduction in the OASIS rate following an OVD simulation curriculum.

